import Applied.Statistics.SPRT

/-!
# Wald's sequential probability ratio test: likelihood-ratio martingale and type-I error bound

- id: stat-sprt-type-one-error
- area: statistics
- status: formalized
- source: A. Wald, "Sequential tests of statistical hypotheses", Ann. Math. Statist. 16 (1945) (Wald's inequality `A ≤ (1-β)/α`, i.e. `α ≤ (1-β)/A`, on the type-I error; equation number to be confirmed; this entry proves only the weaker corollary `α ≤ 1/A`). The proof here goes through Ville's inequality rather than Wald's likelihood-ratio identity.
- used-in: sequential clinical trials, quality control, sequential A/B testing
- prerequisites: Ville's inequality (`Applied/Statistics/Ville.lean`), conditional expectation of a product with an independent factor, densities via `Measure.withDensity`
- statement-review: unreviewed

Informal statement.

(1) Products of independent factors. Let `P` be a probability measure and `Y 0, Y 1, …`
measurable, mutually independent, integrable real random variables. Let `Λ n = ∏_{i<n} Y i`
(so `Λ 0 = 1`) and let the `n`-th σ-algebra of the filtration be the one generated by
`Y 0, …, Y (n-1)`. If every `Y i` is non-negative everywhere and `E[Y i] ≤ 1`, then `Λ` is a
supermartingale. If `E[Y i] = 1` for all `i` (no sign condition needed), then `Λ` is a
martingale and `E[Λ n] = 1` for all `n`. In the supermartingale case, for `A > 0`,
`P(∃ n, Λ n ≥ A) ≤ 1/A`.

(2) Likelihood ratios. Let `ν` be a measure on the observation space, `p₀, p₁` measurable
real functions that are non-negative everywhere, with `p₁` integrable for `ν`. Under the null
measure `P` (a probability measure) the observations `X 0, X 1, …` are measurable, mutually
independent, and each has law `ν` with density `p₀` (`P.map (X i) = ν.withDensity p₀`). Let
`Λ n = ∏_{i<n} p₁(X i) / p₀(X i)`, where the quotient is `0` when `p₀(X i) = 0` (an event of
null probability under `P`).

* If `∫ p₁ dν ≤ 1`, then `Λ` is a supermartingale for the filtration generated by the past
  likelihood-ratio factors, and for every `A > 0`, `P(∃ n, Λ n ≥ A) ≤ 1/A`. Consequently, for
  any random time `τ : Ω → ℕ` (in particular the time at which the SPRT stops, when it
  stops), `P(Λ τ ≥ A) ≤ 1/A`: the SPRT with upper threshold `A` has type-I error at most
  `1/A`, whatever the lower threshold. This is weaker than Wald's bound `α ≤ (1-β)/A`, which
  also involves the type-II error `β`; that sharper bound is not formalized here, but an
  change-of-measure version is in `Targets/Statistics/SPRTWaldChangeOfMeasure.lean`
  (abstractly, and for i.i.d. observations with null density `p₀ > 0`).
* If `∫ p₁ dν = 1` and `p₁ = 0` `ν`-almost everywhere on `{p₀ = 0}`, then `Λ` is a
  martingale and `E[Λ n] = 1` for all `n`.

Conventions and differences from textbook statements. The observations are assumed
independent with common null law, which is what "i.i.d. under the null" means; nothing is
assumed under the alternative, and `p₁` need not be a probability density for the bound (only
`∫ p₁ dν ≤ 1`). The filtration is generated by the likelihood-ratio factors
`p₁(X i)/p₀(X i)`, `i < n`, not by the observations themselves (it is contained in the
filtration of the observations). The textbook SPRT stops at the first exit of `Λ` from an
interval `(B, A)`; the statement here bounds the probability of ever reaching `A`, which
contains the rejection event of every such test, including truncated ones.

Not covered (here; see `SPRTWaldChangeOfMeasure.lean` for the abstract change-of-measure forms of
`α ≤ (1-β)/A` and `β ≤ B(1-α)`): the type-II error bound `β ≤ B` (it is the same statement with the roles of the
two hypotheses exchanged, but is not stated separately); Wald's approximations
`A ≈ (1-β)/α`, `B ≈ β/(1-α)`; almost sure termination; expected sample size (Wald's identity)
and the optimality theorem of Wald and Wolfowitz; composite hypotheses.
-/

